<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christophe Plantin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1520–1589</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christophe Plantin was a French-born printer and publisher who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1549 and built one of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> businesses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His press, the Golden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">Compasses</a>, produced the eight-volume Polyglot Bible for Philip II between 1568 and 1573, besides scientific, humanist and liturgical works. His workshop, run by his Moretus heirs for three centuries, is now the Plantin-Moretus Museum, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christophe-plantin.jpg" alt="Christophe Plantin" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Paul Rubens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_Christoffel_Plantin_-_WGA20356.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
